[;Taylor_series
 Let {f(x)} have derivatives of all order at {x=c},
 The Taylor series of {f(x)} centered at {c} is
 { f(x) = sum__(i=0)^infty frac(1,i!) f^((i))(c) (x-c)^i }
 
 [todo The taylor series of a any polynomial is the polynomial itself
  [For example, for the polynomial { f(x) = x^3 + 2 x^2 + 3 x + 1 }, let's say {c=0}
   { f^((0))(0) = 1 }, which is the constant term
   { f^((1))(0) = (x => 3 x^2 + 4 x + 3)(0) = 3 }, which is the linear coefficient
   { f^((2))(0) = (x => 6 x + 4)(0) = 4 }, which is the quadratic coefficient, multiplied by 2
   { f^((3))(0) = (x => 6)(0) = 4 }, which is the cubic coefficient, multiplied by 6
   
   so you can see how the {frac(1,i!)} plays into this whole story]
  
  [todo What about for arbitrary center {c}?
   ]
  ]
 ]

[;limit_def Suppose {f: R -> R} is a function on the real line,
 we say that the limit of {f(x)} [as {x} approaches {p}] equals {L},
 written { lim__(x->p) f(x) = L }
 when for every output range {epsilon > 0},
 there exists an input range {delta > 0},
 such that for every real {x0} near {x} where
 {0 < abs(x0 - p) < epsilon},
 {abs(f(x0) - L) < epsilon}
 
 [NOTE To {f} to be defined for {x}, since for example
  in the definition of @derivative, {h} can never be zero]
 ]

[;derivative of a function {f} at {a}
 { f'(a) = lim__(h->0) frac(f(a+h) - f(a), h) }
 
 [How do we derive the @derivative of a polynomial?
  suppose {f(x) = x^n }
  {
   f'(a)
   = lim__(h->0) frac((a+h)^n - a^n, h)
  }
  Suppose, for example {f(x) = x^2}
  {
   f'(a)
   = lim__(h->0) frac(a^2 + 2 a h + h^2 - a^2, h)
   = lim__(h->0) frac(2 a h, h)
  }
  As you can see, all terms with higher power of {h} vanish,
  leaving the term {n a^(n-1) h}, so the derivative is {n a^(n-1)}
  ]
 ]

[;chain_rule (tested at @chain_rule_test)
 Suppose {z} depends on {y}, and {y} depends on {x}
 { frac(d z, d x) = frac(d z, d y) frac(d y, d x) }
 
 [#done The proof
  [NOTE We write {frac(d y, d x__0)} for {frac(d y, d x) at x__0}]
  
  Suppose {z = g(y); y = f(x)}
  NOTE Forget about {f,g}, we'll write {z(y) = g(y), y(x) = f(x)}
  
  [Set {y__0 = y(x0)}]
  
  The goal is
  {frac(d z, d x__0) = frac(d z, d y__0) frac(d y, d x__0)}
  
  {  frac(d z, d x__0)
   = lim__(h__x -> 0) frac(z(y(x__0 + h__x)) - z(y(x__0)),
                           h__x)
  }
  
  [#done Now, there are two cases
   [1. When {frac(d y, d x__0) = 0}, then you can see how
    {frac(d z, d x) = 0}, #qed]
   
   [2. When {frac(d y, d x__0) != 0}
    [{small_hx:} There is a range of {0 < h__x < a}, where
     {f(x__0 + h__x) - f(x__0) != 0},
     because {frac(f(x__0 + h__x) - f(x__0), h__x)}
     is supposed to be close to {frac(d y, d x__0)},
     we can set {epsilon < frac(d y, d x__0)}, then
     {frac(f(x__0 + h__x) - f(x__0), h__x) > 0}
     
     [NOTE We will consider only this range]
     [NOTE The proof on wikipedia is stupid about this]
     ]
    
    {lem_89:} With {h__x} in {small_hx}, the stuff in the limit equals
    {  frac(z(y(x__0 + h__x)) - z(y(x__0)),
            h__x)
     = frac(z(y(x__0 + h__x)) - z(y(x__0)),
            y(x__0 + h__x) - y(x__0))
     * frac(y(x__0 + h__x) - y(x__0),
            h__x)
    }
    
    [{lem_99:} We will prove {y(x__0 + h__x)} converges to {y__0}
     as {h__0 -> 0}, when {frac(d y, d x__0)} exists
     
     [Set {L = frac(d y, d x__0)}]
     
     [Given the definition of {frac(d y, d x__0)},
      we can get {abs(frac(y(x__0 + h__x) - y__0, h__x) - L) < epsilon},
      for arbitrary {epsilon}]
     
     [Then we have {abs(y(x__0 + h__x) - y__0 - L abs(h__x)) < abs(h__x epsilon)}]
     [Lemma: {abs(a) - abs(b) <= abs(a-b)}
      You can prove that from {abs(a) + abs(b) >= abs(a+b)},
      set {a -> a-b; b -> b}]
     [So from that lemma, we have
      {abs(y(x__0 + h__x) - y__0) < abs(h__x (epsilon + L))}
      [There you go, you can get {abs(y(x__0 + h__x) - y__0)}
       as small as you want! #qed]]
     ]
    
    [We will prove that {
      lim__(h__x -> 0) frac(z(y(x__0 + h__x)) - z(y(x__0)),
                            y(x__0 + h__x) - y(x__0))
      = frac(d z, d y__0)
     } (and that the limit exists)
     
     [Set {L = frac(d z, d y__0)}]
     
     By definition of {frac(d z, d y__0)}, for small enough {abs(h__y)},
     {abs(frac(z(y__0 + h__y) - z(y__0), h__y) - L) < epsilon}
     
     then according to {lem_99}, we can get {h__x} small enough
     so that {y(x__0 + h__x) - y__0} could be substituted for {h__y}
     the result of which is {
      abs(frac(z(y(x__0 + h__x)) - z(y__0),
               y(x__0 + h__x) - y__0) - L) < epsilon
     }, which is #qed
     ]
    
    Combine that with {lem_89}, this is #qed
    ]
   ]
  ]
 ]

